Delivered: **Alireza averages {{ A.kcal }} kcal / {{ A.p }} g protein / {{ A.f }} g fibre** (days {{ A.lo }}–{{ A.hi }}). **Ana averages {{ N.kcal }} kcal / {{ N.p }} g protein / {{ N.f }} g fibre** (days {{ N.lo }}–{{ N.hi }}). On Saturday, protein is lower (Alireza {{ A.satp }} g, Ana {{ N.satp }} g) because the social dinner is a budget, not a recipe. The weekly averages carry it.

**Health marker:** visceral fat from the Matrix body analysis every 4 weeks. Weight is the secondary signal.

## What changed in v4, and why

| Change | Reason |
|---|---|
| **New menu from your picks.** Dinners: chelo goosht, zereshk polo ba morgh, sabzi polo ba mahi, ghormeh sabzi, homemade pizza, steak night. Lunches: mercimek, chickpea bowl, ash, creamy chicken pasta, adasi, jujeh kabab. Breakfasts: skyr bowl, overnight oats, Persian spread, Ana's omelet | Chosen by you on 7 Nov. Kabab koobideh, gheymeh, burger and bolognese did not fit this month's red-meat budget. They go first into the December menu. |
| **The same five moments every day.** Breakfast · lunch · tea 16:30 · dinner · TV snack 21:30. **No "trained today" toggle and no post-gym shake** | Dinner, eaten soon after the gym, is the post-workout meal. Daily protein total matters more than timing (Schoenfeld 2013 meta-analysis). This frees the calories for an evening snack every day. |
| **Saturday free lunch → Saturday social dinner with a budget** (Alireza ≤ 900, Ana ≤ 550 kcal; drinks inside it every other week) | The free lunch kept spreading into a free day. Putting it at dinner means the day is mostly done when it starts. You chose this. |
| **Evening TV snack every day** (frozen saffron skyr, popcorn or a fruit bowl) and a **tea snack** (dates & walnuts, or apple & peanut butter) | You both want to eat while relaxing. Planned snacks beat unplanned ones. Ana is hungriest at bedtime, so her snack is the last thing she eats. |
| **Treats are built in.** Dark chocolate with tea on weekdays (Alireza 10 g, Ana 6 g). One dessert on Sunday (Ana's baking or bastani, budgeted) | You picked "both, smaller". Rigid all-or-nothing rules are linked to more overeating than flexible ones (Westenhoefer 1999). |
| **Red meat: 3 dinners a week, as you asked.** Alireza {{ A.red }} g raw (~{{ A.redc }} g cooked), Ana {{ N.red }} g raw (~{{ N.redc }} g cooked) | Within the World Cancer Research Fund limit (350–500 g cooked per week). Alireza is above the new Dutch guideline of 200 g a week; Ana is at it. Lean cuts only, no processed meat. |
| **Recipes that work** (cards below). The creamy sauce is thickened with maizena. Pizza dough is real: flour plus skyr. Stews are cooked on Sunday | The old alfredo did not thicken. Ghormeh sabzi needs about 2 hours, which can't happen on a gym evening. |
| **Five legume meals a week, never two in a day** | Fibre: weekly averages of {{ A.f }} g (Alireza) and {{ N.f }} g (Ana). The new Dutch guidance is ~250 g of legumes a week. |
| **Whole packs and tins**: one tin per legume dish, one mozzarella ball, one broccoli head, one spinach bag | Nothing left half-used in the fridge. |
| **Fish:** salmon once a week (sabzi polo ba mahi) | Meets the ~100 g/week oily-fish guidance with the one fish dish you enjoy. |

Removed: post-workout whey + banana, the gym toggle, Saturday free lunch, kwark, passata, knäckebröd, pistolets, kalkoen, forel, fesenjan, alfredo with kwark.
